# No Workflow Drift

Follow the established order of operations and the existing conventions. Do not quietly diverge from
them because a shortcut is convenient.

## Preserve pipeline order

The request pipeline runs in a fixed order: authentication, then authorization, then verification.
Never reorder these stages, and never bypass an earlier stage to reach a later one. A controller or
concern must opt into the pipeline, not route around it.

## Prefer established conventions

Use the conventions the codebase already follows — RESTful routes, path helpers, the existing
authorization mechanism, the existing service and model boundaries. Reach for a new abstraction only
when an existing pattern genuinely does not fit, and say why.

## Rebuild-from-source discipline

When a change is in flight that alters generated or derived state (for example, a schema rebuild
while a rename migration is on a branch), rebuild from source rather than applying incremental
patches on top of a half-changed state. Incremental patches against an inconsistent base drift
further from the intended result.

## Keep behavior deterministic

Given the same inputs, the same path must produce the same outcome. Do not introduce order-, time-,
or environment-dependent branching into the normal flow.
